<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taliesin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taliesin was a Welsh poet of the late 6th century, one of the earliest named poets of the British language. A group of praise poems in the Book of Taliesin, addressed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urien-rheged">Urien of Rheged</a> and his son Owain, are thought by many scholars to be his genuine work. In later medieval legend he became a magical figure, the chief of bards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taliesin.jpg" alt="Taliesin" width="307" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: F. H. Townsend · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_finding_of_Taliesin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
